payments tab kasma sebebi:

Here’s the clear picture—what was wrong, and what we changed to fix it.

What was the problem?

#1 source of jank: a build → post-frame → setState feedback loop
In PaymentsTab.buildList(...) you scheduled:

WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback(...)

which called onFilterApplied(...) → setState(...)

which triggered another build, which scheduled another callback…
This loop was firing every time you switched tabs or filtered, making the UI “stutter”.

Heavy work inside build/row builders

Creating DateFormat('dd.MM.yyyy') and other formatting work per item.

Sorting the whole list every rebuild.

These costs stack up when 20+ items re-render.

Stale item sticking after delete (from console)

Firestore often serves from cache; your UI could see old data.

Flutter can also reuse list elements if keys aren’t stable, so the “ghost” card might linger visually even when the new list is shorter.

Not a hierarchy/dataFuture problem

BaseTab was fine: FutureBuilder gets fresh data and passes it to buildList.

The real issue was side effects during render and reconciliation, not the Future flow.

What did we change to fix it?

Removed the feedback loop entirely

No more addPostFrameCallback in buildList.

onFilterApplied is now a no-op.

We compute summaries (totals/counts) inline during build—pure derivation from filteredItems.
→ Result: one build per data/filter change; no extra frames.

Reduced per-frame/per-row cost

Cached NumberFormat and DateFormat as state fields (created once).

Kept sorting to a single place per build (acceptable), and you can later move it earlier if needed.
→ Result: less work during list paints.

Ensured fresh data & clean reconciliation

Provider now uses query.get(const GetOptions(source: Source.server)) to bypass cache when refreshing.

ListView.builder gets a stable key derived from visible paymentIds; each card uses ValueKey(payment.paymentId).
→ Result: Flutter reliably drops removed items; no “ghost” cards after refresh.

Kept keep-alive, avoided forced remounts

We preserved AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin so tab switching doesn’t reconstruct everything.

We avoided UniqueKey() remounts (those are a last resort and add work).
→ Result: smoother tab switches.

Why did filtering feel “overcomplicated”?

Filtering itself is simple. The complexity came from where it happened:

You mixed state updates into the render phase (build → schedule callback → setState), which causes unnecessary rebuilds and jank.

A clean model is:

Single source of truth: the data from BaseTab’s FutureBuilder.

Filter state: lives in the tab (e.g., _tempStatus, date range, search text).

Filtered list: a pure function of (data, filter state) calculated in build (or a small helper) without setState.

Summaries: computed directly from the filtered list (no extra state).

That’s exactly what we implemented. Now the UI simply reflects (data, filters)—no side-effects and no redundant frames—so it stays smooth even with 20+ items.